dc6a-f48d-4386-8430-469f05fd5059", "0c1e9c26-bbc4-46db-a463-7b9df185d50e", "d7b7be7d-1604-425e-9f1c-8ce3f7f93141", "481a9254-0683-427f-90d7-108911bc0807", "671ff2df-c799-4d8d-b632-481867e1fcca", "856f8902-921d-45c7-8a51-83d31bdf2c92", "f5f2a76c-6024-4215-a1cb-b9af65c14279", "144f8224-9fa4-4d41-a766-afc08038e88b", "a769dbb2-019c-4553-9aaf-4929b5f44eb6", "bf59b076-d31f-43ed-8838-f2a66c6f6fc7", "0839ac76-33e4-4bf0-94ae-effdd28d0338", "9ea7bb75-dcc9-4c59-8d6f-4d731c8c8d00", "17ba5682-02cd-43c0-83dd-ddbba706af1a", "e66d49e3-956d-4a8c-ad72-3a6dfeff0a8e", "6870cb2d-f22b-4afe-a4ee-d3e06011b676", "6e4322d1-bb96-46e2-88ac-ddc7ecbbc13f", "0527c940-8606-49fa-b734-35ce5b25f99d", "c06014f9-0fe8-45ea-a15c-0dca09bafbd4", "8364c220-6714-4b42-8f87-e0b2b5f73476", "2a9b0202-1824-4207-ac70-3872fabac5a1"], "payload": {"kind": "assistant", "text": "Yes, it uses two processes. But there is no separate timer process. The original Python process starts the solver in a child process, waits for its messages until the deadline, and then kills the child. The code is in `runSearchWithDeadline` in [deadlineRunner.py:72](script/sat/deadlineRunner.py:72).\n\n```python\n# Parent: the original Python process, which calls solveBMPSAT.\ndef run_search_with_deadline(time_limit: float) -> Result:\n    best_ub: int = identity_bandwidth           # the identity labeling is the starting answer\n    child: Process = start_process(search_all_bounds)  # spawn a new Python process running the whole bound loop\n    wait_for_message(child)                     # the child says \"ready\" once its imports are done; the clock starts here\n    deadline: float = now() + time_limit\n\n    while True:\n        if not pipe.poll(deadline - now()):     # sleep until a message arrives or the time runs out\n            reason = \"time_limit\"               # no message before the deadline\n            break\n        kind, sent_at, payload = pipe.recv()    # read one message from the child\n        if sent_at > deadline:                  # produced too late: it does not count\n            reason = \"time_limit\"\n            break\n        if kind == \"boundFinished\":\n            if payload.is_sat:\n                best_ub = payload.ub            # keep each checked labeling as soon as it arrives\n            else:\n                status = \"OPTIMAL\"              # UNSAT just below the best bound proves it optimal\n                break\n        ...\n    child.kill()                                # SIGKILL on Linux: stops CaDiCaL even in the middle of a solve\n    child.join()                                # wait until the OS has really ended the child\n    return best_ub, reason\n\n# Child: a separate process that runs the teammate's original loop unchanged.\ndef search_all_bounds(pipe: Connection) -> None:\n    solver: Solver = Solver(\"cadical153\")       # one persistent solver, so incremental solving still works\n    for ub in decreasing_bounds:\n        pipe.send((\"boundStarted\", now(), ub))  # lets the parent record an interrupted bound as \"unknown\"\n        add_clauses(solver, ub)                 # clause construction also runs inside the killed process\n        is_sat: bool = solver.solve()           # can run for a long time; only the parent's kill stops it\n        pipe.send((\"boundFinished\", now(), (ub, is_sat, decode(solver))))\n```\n\nTrace for ibm32 with a 3 s limit and incremental solving (from the measured run):\n- The child finishes bounds 26 down to 12 as SAT. The parent keeps each labeling as it arrives, so `best_ub = 12`.\n- The child starts bound 11 and is still inside `solve()` when the parent's `poll` reaches the deadline.\n- The parent kills the child 3.7 ms after the deadline. It returns FEASIBLE with bandwidth 12, and bound 11 is recorded as unknown, not UNSAT.\n\n**Why not other methods**\n\n`[Observed]` I tested three other ways to stop the solver at the time limit, and none of them works here:\n\n| Method | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Ask CaDiCaL to stop itself (`interrupt()`) | PySAT's CaDiCaL wrapper raises `NotImplementedError` |\n| A timer thread in the same process | It never runs during `solve()`, because the solve holds Python's global lock (GIL) |\n| Run the solver in short pieces and check the clock between them | It changes the search: ibm32 at bound 12 took 6.3 s as one call, but was still unsolved after 25 s in pieces. It also cannot stop clause construction |\n\nOne child process per bound would also work for repeated solving. However, it would destroy the persistent solver that incremental solving depends on, and Task.md requires that the two schemes differ only in the solving scheme. So the whole search runs in one child process for the run.\n\n**Two details beyond the basic idea**\n- The child stamps each message with its send time, and the parent ignores any message stamped after the deadline. This way, a bound that finished late is never counted even if its message arrives before the kill ([deadlineRunner.py:123](script/sat/deadlineRunner.py:123)).\n- If the child crashes, for example by running out of memory, the run raises an error instead of reporting a timeout. On Linux the child is also marked as not dumpable, so a crash ends the process at once instead of being held open about 1.7 s by Ubuntu's crash reporter ([deadlineRunner.py:58](script/sat/deadlineRunner.py:58), [deadlineRunner.py:161](script/sat/deadlineRunner.py:161))."}, "startedAtMs": 1794411776357, "updatedAtMs": 1794411784204}, {"eventIds": ["e419d0eb-5cc5-4b64-b23f-78586fd7be99"], "payload": {"cacheReadTokens": 148755, "cacheWriteTokens": 621, "inputTokens": 149378, "kind": "usage", "outputTokens": 1640}, "startedAtMs": 1794411784216, "updatedAtMs": 1794411784216}, {"eventIds": ["f75487f6-de9f-49df-bff3-b3b55d5faf51"], "payload": {"durationMs": 22287.479188998987, "error": null, "kind": "end", "resume": {"claudeResumeAt": "9a7bc365-e477-4265-9019-d8505ad3e221", "claudeSessionId": "26d30711-7c26-404e-a28b-40b29a6888cd"}, "stopReason": "completed"}, "startedAtMs": 1794411784697, "updatedAtMs": 1794411784697}], "status": "completed", "turnId": "1"}]
